# The preset policy in kgsm-base decides whether the reactor runs on a node; applying it is symlink
# manipulation on disk, so it works during the transaction and in a chroot alike. Starting it is the
# node's post-transaction hook's job, which is where a running system is available.
#
# post_install only. An administrator who disabled this unit keeps it disabled across every later
# version — re-presetting on upgrade would quietly undo that.

post_install() {
    systemctl preset kgsm-reactor.service >/dev/null 2>&1 || true

    cat <<'MSG'

  kgsm-reactor needs no configuration: /etc/kgsm-reactor/kgsm-reactor.env holds overrides only.

  It installs its sample rules into /var/lib/kgsm-reactor/rules.d the first time it starts, one file
  per rule, and they are ordinary rules from that moment — editable, retirable and deletable. An
  upgrade never touches them; the pristine copies stay in /opt/kgsm-reactor/rules.d.

  Every rule ships in `observe` mode, so it records what it would have decided and acts on nothing.
  Read what it made of a week before moving one:

    /opt/kgsm-reactor/kgsm-reactor --decisions --days 7

MSG
}

post_upgrade() {
    cat <<'MSG'

  If pacman wrote a .pacnew beside /etc/kgsm-reactor/kgsm-reactor.env, merge it — new settings are
  declared there and a key the file never declares binds to nothing.

MSG
}
